  <p>Here's a scene that plays out a hundred times a day, on every corner of the web. Someone builds a beautiful, fast, static site — Hugo, Astro, Eleventy, plain HTML, doesn't matter which — and it's <em>great</em>. Pages load in a blink. There's no database to patch, no server to reboot at 2 a.m., no dependency-update Tuesday.</p>

  <p>Then they get to the contact page, or the newsletter signup, or the "request a demo" form, and they hit a wall: <code>&lt;form&gt;</code> tags need somewhere to send their data, and a static site, by definition, has nowhere for that data to go.</p>

  <p>The instinctive fix is to spin up a tiny backend. A Node script. A Python function. Maybe a whole Express app with a database table just for messages. And that's where a lot of otherwise-sensible developers lose an afternoon building infrastructure to solve what is, fundamentally, a five-minute problem.</p>

  <h2>Why a static site can't handle a form by itself</h2>

  <p>A static site is, at its core, a folder of files served exactly as they are. There's no runtime attached to it. When a browser loads <code>index.html</code>, nothing on the hosting side is "listening" for anything; it just hands over bytes.</p>

  <p>A <code>&lt;form&gt;</code> element, on the other hand, is designed around the assumption that <em>something</em> is listening at the URL in its <code>action</code> attribute.</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Write and run your own backend:</strong> Serverless functions, Express, or Docker. (High maintenance).</li>
    <li><strong>Use built-in static host forms:</strong> Platform lock-in, limited feature set.</li>
    <li><strong>Point the form at a dedicated hosted form backend:</strong> Plug and play in 5 minutes. (Recommended).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The 5-Minute Setup</h2>

  <p>Say you have a standard contact form in HTML:</p>

  <pre><code>&lt;form action="https://login.ollastack.com/api/submit/your-form-slug" method="POST"&gt;
  &lt;label&gt;Name &lt;input name="name" required /&gt;&lt;/label&gt;
  &lt;label&gt;Email &lt;input name="email" type="email" required /&gt;&lt;/label&gt;
  &lt;label&gt;Message &lt;textarea name="message" required&gt;&lt;/textarea&gt;&lt;/label&gt;
  &lt;button type="submit"&gt;Send&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;</code></pre>

  <p>Or via JavaScript <code>fetch()</code> for a seamless single-page feel:</p>

  <pre><code>async function handleSubmit(event) &#123;
  event.preventDefault();
  const form = event.target;
  const payload = Object.fromEntries(new FormData(form));
  
  const response = await fetch("https://login.ollastack.com/api/submit/your-form-slug", &#123;
    method: "POST",
    headers: &#123; "Content-Type": "application/json" &#125;,
    body: JSON.stringify(payload),
  &#125;);

  if (response.ok) &#123;
    alert("Thanks for your submission!");
    form.reset();
  &#125;
&#125;</code></pre>

  <h2>Field Conventions</h2>

  <p>Most form backends share magic field names to configure routing:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><code>_replyto</code>: Sets the <code>Reply-To</code> header on the notification email.</li>
    <li><code>_subject</code>: Overrides the notification email subject line.</li>
    <li><code>_next</code>: Custom redirect URL after submission.</li>
    <li><code>_gotcha</code>: Invisible honeypot field to trap spam bots.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Webhooks: Connecting forms to Slack, CRMs &amp; APIs</h2>

  <p>Send submissions anywhere using HMAC-signed webhooks:</p>

  <pre><code>import &#123; createHmac, timingSafeEqual &#125; from "node:crypto";

function verifyWebhook(headers, rawBody, secret) &#123;
  const signatureHeader = headers["x-ollastack-signature"];
  const expected = createHmac("sha256", secret).update(rawBody).digest("hex");
  const provided = Buffer.from(signatureHeader.split("=")[1], "hex");
  const computed = Buffer.from(expected, "hex");
  
  return provided.length === computed.length &amp;&amp; timingSafeEqual(provided, computed);
&#125;</code></pre>

